--- machine\system\cryptography
canonical: Machine\System\Cryptography

The machine's crypto policy, which libpcrypto and libpssl apply to every
program that uses them.

Each use of cryptography is graded as an entry: an algorithm and the
operation it's used for, such as `md5.hmac`, on a ladder from U,
practically broken, to A. `/usr/share/peios-crypto/algorithms.tsv` lists
every entry with its grade, and whether FIPS 140-3 approves it. The
policy refuses entries below one grade, warns about entries below
another, and lets named programs use particular entries through
exceptions. A small floor, such as the null ciphers and RSA keys under
1024 bits, is refused under every policy, and nothing can allow it.

cryptod compiles this key into the policy file libpcrypto reads, when it
starts and whenever the key has been still for a moment after a change.
Each process reads the policy once, at its first use of libpcrypto, and
keeps it: a change applies to processes started afterwards, and a running
service needs a restart to take it up.

If a value here is present but can't be read, such as a grade that isn't
U to A, cryptod keeps the policy it last published and records
`cryptod.policy.failed`, rather than falling back to defaults that could
be looser. If the key doesn't exist, the defaults below apply.

SYSTEM and Administrators can change the key. Every authenticated user can
read it.

--- machine\system\cryptography\reject
canonical: Machine\System\Cryptography\Reject

What the policy refuses.

A refused use fails as if the algorithm were unsupported, with an error
whose reason is 1000 in the `ERR_LIB_USER` library. A refused low-level
digest ends the program, because those functions can't report failure.
Before raising `MinimumGrade`, search `cryptod.algorithm.warned` records
for what would stop working.

--- machine\system\cryptography\reject minimumgrade
canonical: Machine\System\Cryptography\Reject MinimumGrade
type: REG_SZ
default: U
valid: U, D, C, B, A
applies: live (for processes started afterwards)

The lowest grade the policy allows: entries below it are refused.

At `U`, only the floor is refused. `C` refuses everything graded U or D,
such as MD5 and SHA-1 digests (`md5.digest`, `sha1.digest`) and HMAC-MD5
(`md5.hmac`). An exception can allow a
refused entry for particular programs when
`Exceptions\AllowExceptionsToReject` is 1.

--- machine\system\cryptography\reject fips
canonical: Machine\System\Cryptography\Reject FIPS
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0
valid: 0, 1
applies: live (for processes started afterwards)

Whether the policy refuses every entry FIPS 140-3 doesn't approve.

- `0`: approval doesn't matter.
- `1`: refuse unapproved entries, whatever their grade.

Setting it doesn't make the machine operate in a FIPS-approved mode:
libpcrypto's module isn't validated. cryptod's posture always reports
that.

--- machine\system\cryptography\warn
canonical: Machine\System\Cryptography\Warn

What the policy allows but records.

Each process reports the first use of each warned-about entry, and cryptod
records it as `cryptod.algorithm.warned`.

--- machine\system\cryptography\warn minimumgrade
canonical: Machine\System\Cryptography\Warn MinimumGrade
type: REG_SZ
default: C
valid: U, D, C, B, A
applies: live (for processes started afterwards)

The lowest grade the policy allows without recording it: the use of an
entry below it is recorded.

Set it at or above `Reject\MinimumGrade`, or it records nothing that isn't
already refused.

--- machine\system\cryptography\warn fips
canonical: Machine\System\Cryptography\Warn FIPS
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0
valid: 0, 1
applies: live (for processes started afterwards)

Whether the policy records the use of every entry FIPS 140-3 doesn't
approve.

- `0`: approval doesn't matter.
- `1`: record unapproved entries, whatever their grade.

--- machine\system\cryptography\exceptions
canonical: Machine\System\Cryptography\Exceptions

Exceptions to the policy, and how they apply.

Each subkey is one exception, named `Owner.Purpose`. Packages add their own
when they install and remove them when they uninstall.

An exception applies to the programs its `ProgramSecurity` grants, judged
by each process's primary token. Impersonation is ignored: an exception
says which software needs an algorithm, not whose behalf it's acting on.
A service keeps its exceptions while it serves a client, and a client
can't weaken a service's crypto. Any SID can be granted, but a user or
group SID grants every program that runs as that user or group, so prefer
a service's own SID.

Each process's first use of each exception is recorded as
`cryptod.exception.used`, whether or not the exception warns.

--- machine\system\cryptography\exceptions enabled
canonical: Machine\System\Cryptography\Exceptions Enabled
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1
valid: 0, 1
applies: live (for processes started afterwards)

Whether exceptions apply at all.

- `0`: every exception is ignored, so the thresholds apply to every
  program.
- `1`: exceptions apply.

--- machine\system\cryptography\exceptions warnbydefault
canonical: Machine\System\Cryptography\Exceptions WarnByDefault
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0
valid: 0, 1
applies: live (for processes started afterwards)

Whether an exception with no `Warn` value of its own warns about what it
allows.

--- machine\system\cryptography\exceptions warnalways
canonical: Machine\System\Cryptography\Exceptions WarnAlways
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0
valid: 0, 1
applies: live (for processes started afterwards)

Whether every exception warns about what it allows, whatever its own
`Warn` says.

--- machine\system\cryptography\exceptions allowexceptionstoreject
canonical: Machine\System\Cryptography\Exceptions AllowExceptionsToReject
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1
valid: 0, 1
applies: live (for processes started afterwards)

Whether exceptions may allow entries the policy refuses.

- `0`: exceptions only stop warnings. A refused entry is refused for every
  program.
- `1`: an exception allows the entries it names for its programs, except
  the floor.

--- machine\system\cryptography\exceptions\<name>
canonical: Machine\System\Cryptography\Exceptions\<name>

One exception: entries that particular programs may use, named
`Owner.Purpose`, such as `Radius.PacketAuth`.

The name is the owner, a dot, then the purpose, in at most 255 printable
ASCII bytes with no spaces. Packages name their exceptions after
themselves.

An exception that can't apply as written allows nothing. cryptod drops it
whole, publishes the rest of the policy, and records
`cryptod.policy.failed` naming it. That happens when it names an entry
libpcrypto doesn't grade, names one on the floor, names none, or has no
usable `ProgramSecurity`.

--- machine\system\cryptography\exceptions\<name> algorithms
canonical: Machine\System\Cryptography\Exceptions\<name> Algorithms
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (required)
valid: entries in /usr/share/peios-crypto/algorithms.tsv
applies: live (for processes started afterwards)

The entries the exception allows, each an algorithm and an operation, such
as `md5.hmac`.

Name the operation as well as the algorithm. HMAC-MD5 and an MD5 digest
are different entries with different grades, and allowing one doesn't
allow the other. A single REG_SZ entry is accepted too.

--- machine\system\cryptography\exceptions\<name> programsecurity
canonical: Machine\System\Cryptography\Exceptions\<name> ProgramSecurity
type: REG_BINARY or REG_SZ
default: (required)
valid: a security descriptor with an owner and a DACL
applies: live (for processes started afterwards)

The programs that may use the exception: a security descriptor granting
them the Use right, `0x1`.

Give a self-relative descriptor as REG_BINARY, or SDDL as REG_SZ. Each
process's primary token is checked against it, ignoring impersonation.
Grant a service's own SID, `S-1-5-80-...`, for one service, as in
`O:SYG:SYD:(A;;0x1;;;S-1-5-80-...)`. cryptod drops a descriptor with no
owner, which an access check refuses, and one with no DACL, which would
grant every program.

--- machine\system\cryptography\exceptions\<name> warn
canonical: Machine\System\Cryptography\Exceptions\<name> Warn
type: REG_DWORD
default: (absent; Exceptions\WarnByDefault applies)
valid: 0, 1
applies: live (for processes started afterwards)

Whether the exception's use is recorded as a warning as well.

- `0`: only the first use in each process is recorded, as
  `cryptod.exception.used`.
- `1`: each entry's first use is also recorded as
  `cryptod.algorithm.warned`.

`Exceptions\WarnAlways` overrides it.
